[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Bit Manipulation Applications: Counting Set Bits, Checking Power of 2"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Bit manipulation is a powerful technique used to perform operations at the bit level. It can lead to efficient solutions for various problems, especially in areas like data structures and algorithms. This tutorial explores two common applications: counting set bits and checking if a number is a power of 2."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Counting Set Bits (Hamming Weight)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The number of set bits (bits that are '1') in a binary representation of a number is also known as its Hamming weight. Counting set bits has applications in areas like error correction, cryptography, and population counting."},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"A naive approach would be to iterate through each bit of the number and check if it's set. However, bit manipulation offers more efficient solutions."},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"// C++ implementation of counting set bits using Brian Kernighan's Algorithm
#include <iostream>

int countSetBits(int n) {
  int count = 0;
  while (n) {
    n &= (n - 1); // Clear the least significant set bit
    count++;
  }
  return count;
}

int main() {
  int num = 7; // Example number
  std::cout << "Number of set bits in " << num << " is: " << countSetBits(num) << std::endl;
  return 0;
}"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Brian Kernighan's Algorithm is an efficient way to count set bits. The expression 'n &= (n - 1)' clears the least significant set bit in 'n'. The loop continues until 'n' becomes 0. The number of iterations is equal to the number of set bits."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Checking if a Number is a Power of 2"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"A number is a power of 2 if it can be expressed as 2 raised to some non-negative integer power (e.g., 1, 2, 4, 8, 16...). Bit manipulation provides a concise way to determine if a number is a power of 2."},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"A power of 2 in binary representation has only one bit set (e.g., 2 is 10, 4 is 100, 8 is 1000). Therefore, we can use bitwise AND to check if a number is a power of 2."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"// C++ implementation to check if a number is a power of 2
#include <iostream>

bool isPowerOfTwo(int n) {
  if (n <= 0) {
    return false; // 0 and negative numbers are not powers of 2
  }
  return (n & (n - 1)) == 0;
}

int main() {
  int num1 = 8; // Example 1
  int num2 = 6; // Example 2
  std::cout << num1 << " is a power of 2: " << std::boolalpha << isPowerOfTwo(num1) << std::endl;
  std::cout << num2 << " is a power of 2: " << std::boolalpha << isPowerOfTwo(num2) << std::endl;
  return 0;
}"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"The expression '(n & (n - 1))' clears the least significant set bit in 'n'. If 'n' is a power of 2, it will have only one set bit. Clearing that bit will result in 0. Therefore, if '(n & (n - 1))' is 0, then 'n' is a power of 2. We also need to check if n is positive, as 0 and negative numbers are not powers of 2."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Bit manipulation can significantly improve the performance of certain algorithms."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Understanding bitwise operators is crucial for effective bit manipulation."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Practice applying bit manipulation techniques to various problems to enhance your problem-solving skills."}]
